<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qizil Arslan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1191">1191</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Uthman Qizil Arslan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qizil Arslan was an atabeg of the Eldiguzid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, the son of Eldiguz, who succeeded his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahan-pahlavan">Jahan Pahlavan</a> in 1186. He fought the last Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghrul-iii">Toghrul III</a>, imprisoned him and proclaimed himself sultan, but was murdered in 1191. Like his family he was a patron of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qizil-arslan.jpg" alt="Qizil Arslan" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1200s artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkic_emir_Lord_Muzaffar_al-Dawla_wal-Din_in_a_battle_at_Khalkh%C4%81l._1185-1210_CE_(Freer_Gallery_of_Art,_F1943.3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
